"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,928\nThe Learning Brain\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,562\nEpisode 14\n\n3\n00:00:07,642 --> 00:00:09,673\nStress and emotion\n\n4\n00:00:12,473 --> 00:00:15,955\nTwo versions of a slide show tell nearly the same story.\n\n5\n00:00:16,403 --> 00:00:19,468\nIn one, a boy visits a hospital with his mother.\n\n6\n00:00:19,880 --> 00:00:22,228\nIn the other, he is badly hurt on the way,\n\n7\n00:00:22,248 --> 00:00:23,736\nand rushed into surgery.\n\n8\n00:00:24,258 --> 00:00:27,787\nA week later, people remember the emotional version better,\n\n9\n00:00:27,905 --> 00:00:29,221\nespecially its middle.\n\n10\n00:00:29,713 --> 00:00:32,196\nBlock adrenaline's effects with a drug,\n\n11\n00:00:32,369 --> 00:00:34,468\nand that advantage disappears.\n\n12\n00:00:34,638 --> 00:00:37,746\nEmotional arousal releases stress hormones.\n\n13\n00:00:38,098 --> 00:00:43,626\nThe amygdala responds, and signals the hippocampus to store the moment more strongly.\n\n14\n00:00:44,120 --> 00:00:47,211\nArousal tags an experience as worth keeping.\n\n15\n00:00:47,381 --> 00:00:49,475\nBut the same chemistry has a cost.\n\n16\n00:00:49,869 --> 00:00:54,734\nEven mild, uncontrollable stress quickly impairs the prefrontal cortex,\n\n17\n00:00:54,979 --> 00:00:57,471\nthe home of working memory and flexible thought,\n\n18\n00:00:57,782 --> 00:01:00,684\nwhile strengthening fast emotional responses.\n\n19\n00:01:01,140 --> 00:01:07,217\nA meta-analysis of one hundred thirteen studies found that stress just after learning can help memory.\n\n20\n00:01:07,740 --> 00:01:11,181\nStress just before or during recall impairs it.\n\n21\n00:01:11,351 --> 00:01:13,653\nThat is why test anxiety hurts.\n\n22\n00:01:14,020 --> 00:01:16,822\nWorries take up the working memory the test needs.\n\n23\n00:01:17,166 --> 00:01:18,683\nIn one classroom study,\n\n24\n00:01:18,889 --> 00:01:23,606\nninth graders wrote for ten minutes about their worries just before a final exam.\n\n25\n00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:28,438\nThe most anxious students then did about as well as their calmer classmates.\n\n26\n00:01:28,776 --> 00:01:34,477\nAnd students told that their nerves could help them scored higher on the math section of the GRE.\n\n27\n00:01:34,647 --> 00:01:35,514\nCautions.\n\n28\n00:01:35,883 --> 00:01:37,293\nThe familiar inverted U,\n\n29\n00:01:37,313 --> 00:01:40,351\nwhere a little arousal helps and too much hurts,\n\n30\n00:01:40,559 --> 00:01:42,011\nis a useful sketch,\n\n31\n00:01:42,221 --> 00:01:43,623\nnot a precise law.\n\n32\n00:01:44,111 --> 00:01:46,008\nSome emotional memory findings,\n\n33\n00:01:46,028 --> 00:01:47,747\nincluding the drug study,\n\n34\n00:01:47,931 --> 00:01:49,690\nhave not always replicated.\n\n35\n00:01:50,056 --> 00:01:53,378\nAnd chronic stress is a different problem from a tense exam.\n\n36\n00:01:53,548 --> 00:01:54,288\nFor teaching.\n\n37\n00:01:54,648 --> 00:01:56,454\nAim for challenge without threat.\n\n38\n00:01:56,869 --> 00:01:58,917\nKeep stakes clear and predictable.\n\n39\n00:01:59,214 --> 00:02:01,847\nSpread grades across many small assessments,\n\n40\n00:02:01,867 --> 00:02:03,766\nrather than a few huge ones.\n\n41\n00:02:04,182 --> 00:02:08,155\nBefore big exams, give students a few minutes to write about their worries,\n\n42\n00:02:08,262 --> 00:02:11,386\nand tell them that nerves are energy they can use.\n\n43\n00:02:11,768 --> 00:02:14,042\nEmotion can make material memorable.\n\n44\n00:02:14,386 --> 00:02:16,041\nThreat makes it harder to think.\n\n45\n00:02:16,211 --> 00:02:18,371\nArousal helps memory keep a moment.\n\n46\n00:02:18,701 --> 00:02:20,452\nThreat takes thinking offline.\n\n47\n00:02:20,756 --> 00:02:22,562\nAim for challenge, not threat.\n\n"
